WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.584
Graph neural networks are being deployed in
high-stakes applications where the test-time

00:00:05.664 --> 00:00:08.159
data rarely matches training conditions.

00:00:08.239 --> 00:00:13.244
In these shifted settings, downstream safety
metrics such as calibration error,

00:00:13.324 --> 00:00:15.175
out-of-distribution rejection,

00:00:15.255 --> 00:00:20.324
and generalization gap prediction all rely on the
model's confidence indicators.

00:00:20.404 --> 00:00:27.598
While the computer vision community has long known
that confidence quality deteriorates under distribution shift,

00:00:27.678 --> 00:00:31.975
this behavior has remained under-explored for graph
neural networks.

00:00:32.055 --> 00:00:38.283
This paper asks whether GNN confidence indicators
can be made reliable under realistic structural,

00:00:38.363 --> 00:00:40.600
size, concept, and covariate shifts.
